WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.460
THE NETFLIX DEAL:
On October 31, 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:30.480
Carl Icahn announced that he had purchased&nbsp;
a 10% stake in Netflix. This was really a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.120
Halloween spook for Netflix as Icahn was somone&nbsp;
that you didn’t want anywhere near your company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:43.200
People like Icahn constantly assure you that oh&nbsp;
it’s just a passive investment, I’m not trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:51.180
takeover the company, and then boom you get sued&nbsp;
for depicting Cleopatra as Black. Wait nevermind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:59.220
wrong result. Boom, Icahn shows up with enough&nbsp;
money to buy you out cash. Back in 2012, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:06.720
wasn’t even that hard as Netflix was only worth $4&nbsp;
to $5 billion which was nothing for Carl Icahn. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:14.160
had orchestrated $8 billion buyouts way back in&nbsp;
the 80s, so $4 billion in 2012 was childs play.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.440
With a net worth of $21 billion, he&nbsp;
could literally buy out Netflix with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.660
personal loan and that was probably what he was&nbsp;
planning on doing despite his hollow promises.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.200
Netflix’s executive team would scramble trying to&nbsp;
find a way to fend off this bully, and they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.680
end up implementing a defense called the poison&nbsp;
pill. The poison pill was somewhat effective but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:44.220
it was by no means a full proof plan. All Netflix&nbsp;
could do though was nervously wait and hope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:51.060
they don’t hear about another purchase. But, weeks&nbsp;
went by, months went by, and a full years went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:59.100
buy. No new purchases. In fact, Icahn would even&nbsp;
go ahead sell off his entire stake by mid 2015.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:07.320
Had the poison pill really worked? Had Netflix&nbsp;
just fended off the infamous Carl Icahn? No way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.120
Icahn was actually being honest about&nbsp;
his passive intentions right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:18.780
probably not and I don’t think he was put off by&nbsp;
the poison pill either. The reality is that Icahn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:25.080
had lost his mojo. While his net worth had gone&nbsp;
up over the years, his days in power toying with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:31.440
fortune 10 companies were long gone. It wasn’t&nbsp;
just Icahn who was in this situation. Corporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:37.200
raiders as a whole had lost their power. Back&nbsp;
in the day, these guys were the worst nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.540
of small companies and their shareholders. With&nbsp;
financing from Wall Street, raiders would march&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:48.120
into random companies where they were not welcome&nbsp;
and buyout a large portion of the company if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:54.060
the entire company. After that, they would go onto&nbsp;
fire large portions of the workforce, increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.520
efficiency no matter the cost, and bolster&nbsp;
the stock price through any means possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:04.740
Once the company has been milked, raiders will&nbsp;
happily sell off the remaining pulp of the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:10.740
to some random sucker before moving onto to their&nbsp;
next victim. This was the brash and ruthless job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:19.140
of a corporate raider. But while this was no doubt&nbsp;
a nasty job, it was also lucrative. Till recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:26.040
Icahn was worth over $20 billion. But, today,&nbsp;
he’s fighting against accusations that he’s been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:32.460
running a Ponzi which has crushed his net worth&nbsp;
down to just under $8 billion. So, here’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:38.700
insane story of how Icahn went from playing with&nbsp;
Fortune 10 companies to being accused of fraud.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.520
CARL ICAHN:&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:51.840
Taking a look back, corporate raiding doesn’t have&nbsp;
an extensive history like stock buybacks or dual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:58.260
class stock. It rose in popularity during an era&nbsp;
and then disappeared shortly after. There were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:03.420
few instances of corporate raiding throughout the&nbsp;
late 1960s, but corporate raiding didn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:09.300
become a thing until Carl Icahn. Icahn started&nbsp;
off as nothing more than an average kid born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:16.440
in 1946 to a Jewish family in Brooklyn New York.&nbsp;
His family was squarely in the middle class with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.360
both of Icahn’s parents being school teachers.&nbsp;
But this didn’t hold him back from getting into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:27.780
Princeton University and graduating with a degree&nbsp;
in philosophy in 1957. At first, it seems that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:32.820
wanted to be a doctor as he would enroll in&nbsp;
the NYU School of Medicine but he would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:37.860
up dropping out and joining the military reserve&nbsp;
forces. He would follow this up with a traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:44.580
job on Wall Street as a stock broker in 1961. He&nbsp;
would learn the ins and outs of stocks, options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:48.720
bonds, warrants, and other financial products&nbsp;
over the next 7 years before opening his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.380
company called Icahn &amp; Co in 1968. From the very&nbsp;
beginning, Icahn was especially interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:01.680
leveraging market inefficiencies but he was by&nbsp;
no means buying out entire companies. Rather his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.680
company was focused on something called arbitrage&nbsp;
trading. This is basically when you make money by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.600
leveraging discrepancies in market pricing between&nbsp;
different markets and exchanges. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:19.500
Tesla stock might be worth $100 on TD Ameritrade&nbsp;
but might be worth $101 on Robinhood. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.420
case, it would make sense to buy Tesla stock on&nbsp;
TD and then instantly sell the stake on Robinhood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:30.720
You would only be making $1 per share but if you&nbsp;
did it with 100,000 shares, well now you’re making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:37.080
serious money. That’s just an example though as&nbsp;
it’s not nearly that easy. Such blatant pricing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:43.140
discrepancies are extremely rare. You’d be lucky&nbsp;
to find a 5 or 10 cent discrepancy on popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:49.140
stocks. But, you’ll have a much better chance&nbsp;
if you look deeper than just common stock. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:54.540
example, you could look at the debt market. Many&nbsp;
companies issue bonds that can be converted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:00.600
stock at a set ratio. So, theoretically, you&nbsp;
could buy a Tesla convertible bond and convert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:05.640
it into 10 shares of Tesla stock. If your all in&nbsp;
cost for the bond is less than the current price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:12.420
of 10 Tesla shares, well then, you’re golden. You&nbsp;
can just sell the 10 shares and make money. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:17.640
is precisely how Icahn made his initial money. He&nbsp;
was constantly looking for discrepancies between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:22.860
options, preferred stock, common stock,&nbsp;
bonds, warrants, and mutual funds and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:29.040
was constantly converting between these assets to&nbsp;
generate a profit. Many people aren’t big fans of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:35.280
market arbitrage as they see people like Icahn as&nbsp;
bottom feeders. They basically scrape by and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.160
a living without adding any real value to the&nbsp;
world. The argument against this would be that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:47.280
arbitrageurs make markets more efficient. But, I&nbsp;
don’t think Icahn was concerned about justifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.320
his means to anyone as it wasn’t long until he&nbsp;
jumped into something much more controversial.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.780
A NEW PRACTICE:&nbsp;

00:06:54.780 --> 00:07:04.680
Much of the popularity of corporate&nbsp;
raiding can be explained by the economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:10.440
environment throughout the 70s and 80s. Interest&nbsp;
rates were extraordinarily high at up to 19%. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:14.400
first glance, this might sound like a great&nbsp;
thing as you could just store away cash in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:20.880
a savings account and earn Warren Buffett level&nbsp;
returns until you consider inflation. Inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:27.840
also reached up to 15% so rarely were you making&nbsp;
any sort of real return. This didn’t just apply to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:33.000
savings accounts either. Whether you were looking&nbsp;
at real estate or stocks or gold or index funds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:40.020
chances were high that there was no real return.&nbsp;
This made investors especially desperate and risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.700
tolerant. They were willing to do just about&nbsp;
anything including piling into junk bonds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:53.400
In fact, between 1979 and 1989 the junk bond&nbsp;
market exploded from $10 billion to $189 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.300 --> 00:08:00.360
But, our man Icahn had a much better pitch for&nbsp;
investors. Given that people were shying away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:06.120
from stocks, the market had stagnated for about&nbsp;
10 years and many blue chip stocks were heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:12.060
discounted. This was a great opportunity for value&nbsp;
investors like Warren Buffett but Icahn didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:17.580
stop at just investing in these value companies.&nbsp;
Icahn would purchase a controlling share or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:23.400
entire company and he would quote on quote&nbsp;
“optimize the company”. By optimize, he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.600
basically strip these companies down to their&nbsp;
bare bones and eliminate anything that wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:33.240
generating a profit. This might not sound that bad&nbsp;
until you realize just what he was eliminating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:41.400
For example, he might go in and kill a company’s&nbsp;
R&amp;D department. This would make the company more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:47.940
profitable than ever for the next 3 to 5 years&nbsp;
but after that, they’re absolutely screwed. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:52.920
was none of Icahn’s concern though as he would’ve&nbsp;
already flipped the business by this point. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:57.300
essentially, Icahn and his fellow corporate&nbsp;
raiders were basically going around and ruining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:03.600
companies for personal gain. Icahn’s first target&nbsp;
was an appliance company called Tappan. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:09.000
acquire a controlling stake in the company in&nbsp;
1978 and do some gutting. He would then force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:15.420
the company to be sold to Electrolux netting him&nbsp;
a gross profit $2.7 million or a 100% return.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:22.920
With a taste of success, Icahn would quickly jump&nbsp;
up to playing with the tens of millions. In 1983&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:28.560
for example, he would acquire ACF industries and&nbsp;
flip it to Philips Petroleum in 1985 for a $50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:34.980
million profit. At least with those two instances,&nbsp;
Icahn was simply flipping these companies. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:39.780
other instances, he would straight up blackmail&nbsp;
these companies as was the case with Saxon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:45.660
industries. Icahn bought a stake in Saxonin 1980&nbsp;
and threatened that he was going to find someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:52.020
to buy the entire company. This forced the company&nbsp;
to buyout Icahn’s shares which netted him a quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:59.700
$2 million. So, there was no doubt that Icahn was&nbsp;
wreaking havoc quite early on but no one really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:06.360
fought him. The companies that he was going after&nbsp;
were no name brands with not much support. Surely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:12.720
he couldn’t pull this off with a true giant. Or&nbsp;
atleast, that’s what they thought until he did.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:20.040
THE BIG LEAGUES:&nbsp;

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:25.860
In 1985, Icahn would pool together all of his&nbsp;
investor funds and max out his bank credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:32.580
lines to purchase a 20% stake in Trans World&nbsp;
Airlines for approximately $70 million. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:39.120
of you probabaly aren’t familiar with Trans World&nbsp;
Airlines or TWA well because Icahn would run it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:45.720
the ground but we’ll get to that. When Icahn&nbsp;
purchased his stake, TWA was the 4th largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:52.440
airline in the US. In fact, their planes alone&nbsp;
were worth over a billion dollars which was big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:57.960
stuff for 1985. This isn’t to say that they&nbsp;
were perfect. They were dealing with issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:03.660
with unions and trying to stage a turnaround but&nbsp;
Icahn’s presence ensured that this turnaround&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:09.420
never came. Just a few months after his initial&nbsp;
purchase, Icahn would up his stake to just over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:16.860
50% which gave him control of TWA. And a few years&nbsp;
later, Icahn would take TWA completely private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:23.040
upping his stake to 90%. During a normal corporate&nbsp;
turnaround, businesses woud focus on eliminating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.680
the bad parts of the business and doubling down&nbsp;
on the good parts, but Icahn would do the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:34.320
opposite. He would eliminate the good parts of&nbsp;
the business by selling them off. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:40.500
he would sell off their popular London routes to&nbsp;
American Airlines for $445 million. Pretty soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:46.020
TWA had nothing left but losses and they&nbsp;
would unsurprisingly file for bankruptcy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:53.580
in early 1992 with $540 million in debt. This&nbsp;
didn’t really matter for Icahn though as he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.580 --> 00:12:00.240
already secured $469 million worth of profit&nbsp;
for himself. The story of TWA definitely made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:05.100
some waves on the news cycles back in the&nbsp;
80s but it wasn’t Icahn that really turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:10.380
everyone against corporate raiders. That honor&nbsp;
actually goes to one of Icahn’s biggest rivals:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:17.040
T Boone Pickens. In the early 1980s, Pickens&nbsp;
would launch a hostile takeover against Gulf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:24.540
Oil. This was absolutely unprecedented because&nbsp;
Gulf Oil wasn’t just a massive company, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:31.740
straight up a fortune 10 company. Fortunately, the&nbsp;
Standard Oil Company of California or Chevron was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:36.900
able to step in and buyout the entire company for&nbsp;
$13 billion which prevented Pickens from wreaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:44.100
any havoc. But while this salvaged Gulf Oil,&nbsp;
it also gave Pickens a profit of $760 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:51.120
Pretty soon, Icahn was playing with mega billion&nbsp;
dollar companies as well. In 1986, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:56.940
he launched an $8 billion takeover of US&nbsp;
Steel. He never ended up taking over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:01.080
entire company but that didn’t stop&nbsp;
him from making a cool $200 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:07.560
His biggest profit however came in 1989 when&nbsp;
he sold his $2 billion stake in Texaco for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:15.660
$700 million profit. Things had gone way too far&nbsp;
and enough was enough. These tycoons were playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:21.600
around with Fortune 10 Fortune 100 companies&nbsp;
as if they were lemonade stands. Companies were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:27.300
understandably not just annoyed but triggered,&nbsp;
and pretty soon, they would start fighting back.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.800
COMPANIES FIGHT BACK:&nbsp;

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:40.440
One of the first defenses that came about against&nbsp;
corporate raiders was the Poison Pill defense. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:45.360
may have heard about this with Twitter as they&nbsp;
were indeed using this defense. This defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:51.060
basically consists of diluting the corporate&nbsp;
raider into oblivion by issuing a crap ton of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:55.980
shares. This way, the corporate raider is never&nbsp;
able to acquire majority control of the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.980 --> 00:14:01.740
through the public markets. This is actually how&nbsp;
Netflix defended themselves against Icahn. Right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:06.780
after Icahn purchased a 10% stake in Netflix,&nbsp;
Netflix would adopt a poison pill defense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:13.080
This was successful at warding off Icahn from&nbsp;
buying out Netflix but this didn’t stop Icahn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:20.220
from making $800 million nonetheless though he&nbsp;
would’ve made $40 billion if he held on. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:26.040
another popular strategy that companies adopted&nbsp;
was golden parachutes. This doesn’t actually do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:31.920
anything to save the company or shareholders&nbsp;
but it does save the executives. Essentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:36.720
golden parachutes allow for executives to walk&nbsp;
away with massive payouts if the company gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:42.840
acquired. Twitter’s executives for example walked&nbsp;
away with over a $100 million in severance. To be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:48.000
honest, I wouldn’t call this a defense.&nbsp;
This is more a case of executives saving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:53.760
themselves. A more traditional defense though is&nbsp;
the White Knight Defense. We briefly touched on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:59.220
this actually with Gulf Oil and T Boone Pickens.&nbsp;
Remember how Gulf Oil sold themselves to Cheveron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:04.740
to avoid Pickens? That’s a white knight defense,&nbsp;
selling yourself to a friendly party to avoid a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:10.680
takeover from a hostile party. There are a bunch&nbsp;
of other defenses like the Greenmail Defense and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:15.720
the Employee Stock Ownership Defense. And many&nbsp;
of these have been used successfully to defend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:22.260
companies from Texaco and Gucci to Netflix and&nbsp;
Twitter. But, none of these strategies really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:27.720
took down corporate raiding. Sure, they made&nbsp;
corporate raiding harder but they by no means made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:34.080
it impossible or non lucrative. Heck, the richest&nbsp;
man in the world today, Bernard Arnault, made most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:39.240
of his fortune by raiding the fashion industry&nbsp;
throughout the 80s and 90s. And Elon literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:45.300
just did one with Twitter, so it’s definitely&nbsp;
still possible. But then, why has corporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:52.380
raiding become so rare? Well, the primary reason&nbsp;
is because investors walked away. Back in the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.380 --> 00:16:00.600
1900s, making a 100% return within 3 or 4 years&nbsp;
was unheard of but thanks to tech stocks, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:06.960
been largely normalized. This isn’t to say that a&nbsp;
100% return within 3 to 4 years isn’t a phenomenal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:14.460
return. It is a phenomenal return but there’s far&nbsp;
more opportunities to make such returns. Meta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:21.720
for example, has recovered nearly 200% within the&nbsp;
past 6 months itself. Why be the villain, destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:27.180
companies, and take on so much risk when you can&nbsp;
just bet on up and coming blue chip companies and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:35.700
make the same or even better returns with far less&nbsp;
stress? The answer is no reason at all. All of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:39.780
people who would’ve backed someone like Icahn in&nbsp;
the 80s have simply turned to backing someone like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:46.080
Zuckerberg or Pichai or Bitcoin. And that right&nbsp;
there is what truly killed corporate raiding.

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:53.340
A NEW CONTROVERSY:&nbsp;

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:59.280
Today, Icahn is plagued with a completely&nbsp;
new controversy. At the beginning of May,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:04.140
Hindenberg research accused that Icahn has&nbsp;
been paying out dividends using funds from new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:09.360
investors. And given that Hindenberg has a pretty&nbsp;
good track record from taking down Nikola motors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:15.120
to taking down the Adani Group, it’s very possible&nbsp;
that these accusations are indeed legitimate but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:21.300
only time will tell. In the end, corporate raiding&nbsp;
was an invention that came from investors’ desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:27.360
for outsized returns over everything else. This&nbsp;
allowed for several raiders to rise to prominence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:30.960
who played with billion dollar companies&nbsp;
on a regular basis as they made billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:36.780
for themselves and their investors. Eventually,&nbsp;
companies started catching on and started fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:42.300
back with varying levels of success. There’s&nbsp;
also an argument to be made that banks become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:47.880
more reluctant to lend to corporate raiders as&nbsp;
a lot of raiders simply failed. But, really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:55.080
the bigger trend that killed off corporate raiding&nbsp;
was the rise of tech. Investors simply found a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.080 --> 00:18:00.060
shiny thing to invest in. Why flip appliance&nbsp;
and oil companies when you can bet on tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:06.420
companies that reach billions of people. And that&nbsp;
is what marked the end of the hunt for Carl Icahn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:13.200
the infamous legend who tried to buy Netflix. Do&nbsp;
